# The future tense puts `will` before a verb (`running make will build
# the binary`). The reader cannot tell whether the sentence describes
# a later event or the system's normal behavior. The technical-writing
# skill writes the present tense for what the system does and keeps
# `will` for a later event. A word rule cannot tell the two apart, but
# most hits describe normal behavior.
#
# The rule also reports `will be` plus a participle, the `-ing` or `-ed`
# form of a verb (`the default handler will be used`), which the passive
# rule leaves to it. The example in the message rewrites such a sentence
# in the present tense with the actor as the subject (`the kubelet uses
# the default handler`), so one fix clears it.
#
# The filter narrows the finding to `will`, so a rule that reports the
# words after it keeps its own finding (`will give up`). A sentence that
# opens with `Will` is a question, and the filter needs a word before
# `will`, so it passes. The guard skips `we will`, which the signpost
# rule reports (`we will explore`), and `time will tell`, which the
# closing-cliche rule reports. It also skips `will want` and `will need
# to`, which the obligation rule reports. `will need` before a noun
# still counts (`you will need Docker`). The roadmap rule reports `will
# be added`, `will be supported`, `will be removed`, and `will be
# deprecated`, and the wordy rule reports `will be able to`, so the
# guard skips those too. prose-lint turns the rule off for a commit
# message, because a commit message describes what the system does
# after the change lands.
#
# It hit 2169 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# described the system's normal behavior.
expr skip [we, time]
expr roadmap [added, supported, removed, deprecated]
expr main <(!@skip will [<VERB, ![want, need]>, (need !to), (be ![@roadmap, able])]), will>
let message "Future tense. Write the present tense for what the system does, such as `the kubelet uses the default handler`."
let description "Flags `will` before a verb, where the present tense usually describes what the system does."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Running make builds the binary."
allows "Will the job retry?"
allows "We will explore the scheduler."
allows "Only time will tell."
allows "The flag will be removed in the next release."
allows "You will want to pin the version."
allows "The caller will be able to cancel the request."
allows "The job will need to restart."
